ncoding/json\"\n+\t\"io\"\n \t\"net/http\"\n \t\"net/http/httptest\"\n \t\"os\"\n \t\"path/filepath\"\n","additions":1,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"main_test.go","time":{"start":1782046132725,"end":1782046132727}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The panic happens in `do` when `body` is nil and we try to call `.Len()` on it. In `net/http.NewRequest`, the body `*bytes.Buffer` calls `.Len()` if it's not nil... actually the panic is in `bytes.(*Buffer).Len` called from `NewRequestWithContext`. That means http.NewRequest is checking the buffer's Len on a nil buffer pointer.\n\nWait, in my `do` function:\n```go\nfunc do(t *testing.T, method, url string, body *bytes.Buffer) *http.Response {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tvar b *bytes.Buffer\n\tif body != nil {\n\t\tb = body\n\t}\n\treq, err := http.NewRequest(method, url, b)\n```\n\nWhen b is nil, `http.NewRequest` accepts an `io.Reader` interface. But `*bytes.Buffer` implements `io.Reader`. A nil `*bytes.Buffer` is a non-nil interface value (with nil concrete value). The http.NewRequest then checks if the body is a `*bytes.Buffer` to get its Len, which causes the panic.\n\nI need to pass `nil` explicitly as `io.Reader`, not a nil `*bytes.Buffer`. Let me change `do` to take `io.Reader`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eea3a0249001qksSD4UvgJ20RG","sessionID":"ses_115c93e67ffeFqZgeAH4FYd0MW","messageID":"msg_eea39f136001CWrArNjVErQiy7"}}
