on\n{\"title\":\"Clean Code (2nd Edition)\",\"year\":2022}\n```\n\nBut validation requires author. The `bookInput.validate()` rejects missing author. The PUT spec says \"Update a book\" — but our validation is strict for create. The test expects PUT to allow partial update without requiring author (since author already exists in DB).\n\nI need to relax validation for update. Or better: change validate to be used differently for create vs update. For PUT, since we're updating an existing record, only validate fields that ARE provided (e.g., if author is provided, must be non-empty; if not provided, OK because we keep the existing value).\n\nLet me adjust the validation logic: create a separate `validateForUpdate` that only validates provided fields. Or change `validate()` so that for nil fields it's OK (just don't add to errs), but for provided-but-empty fields, error.\n\nWait actually my current validate() does this for year and isbn — only errors if non-nil and bad. For title and author though, it requires them to be present. For create they must be present; for update, they can be absent (partial update).\n\nBest approach: make the validate function take a flag, or have a separate validateUpdate function. Let me split it.\n\nActually, simpler: I'll allow PUT to be partial. The handler for update will validate only present fields. Let me add a `validateOptional` method.\n\nLet me restructure models.go:\n- `validate()` — strict, used for create (title & author required)\n- `validatePartial()` — only validates non-nil fields, used for update\n\nBut wait — the test integration sends `{\"title\":\"...\", \"year\":...}` without author and expects 200 OK. So update with missing author must succeed. Use validatePartial for update.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eea3abe550017jEn3LYxi4nefX","sessionID":"ses_115c93e67ffeFqZgeAH4FYd0MW","messageID":"msg_eea3a2b45001xTyd2nngC4HigF"}}
